.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt ABSD-SFTP 8
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm absd-sftp
.Nd bounded SSH file transfer subsystem
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The SSH server starts
.Nm
for an authenticated
.Li sftp
subsystem request without a terminal.
It uses the session's filesystem access and ends when the connection ends.
It has no separate listener or configuration file.
Relative paths and the empty path start at
.Pa /home/operator .
.Pp
Run OpenSSH's clients on the client computer, using the same login key,
host key pinning, and reachable endpoint as the SSH login.
Replace HOST and PORT below with that endpoint:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sftp -P PORT -i operator-key operator@HOST
sftp> put local.bin /home/operator/remote.bin
sftp> get /home/operator/remote.bin returned.bin
sftp> quit
$ scp -P PORT -i operator-key local.bin operator@HOST:/home/operator/remote.bin
$ scp -P PORT -i operator-key operator@HOST:/home/operator/remote.bin returned.bin
.Ed
.Pp
For a headless host, use the established authenticated access path.
A host-local loopback address is usable only on the computer where the
forwarded port actually listens.
No terminal should be allocated for file transfers.
Check important transfers with
.Xr sha256sum 1 .
.Ss Supported requests
SFTP version 3: open, positional read and write, close, file attributes,
directory open and listing, mkdir, rmdir, remove, rename, and realpath.
Size-only attribute updates truncate or extend a writable file.
This includes the final size update used by ordinary modern
.Nm scp
when replacing a longer destination with a shorter or empty file.
Rename refuses an existing destination without replacing it.
Realpath can resolve a missing final name when its existing parent resolves;
that response establishes neither existence nor access to the final name.
.Pp
Creation permission attributes are advisory.
Reported mode bits describe object type and readonly state;
filesystem mounts determine access.
The session cannot read
.Pa /ssh
or write through readonly mounts such as
.Pa /etc .
See
.Xr filesystem 7
and
.Xr handles 7 .
.Ss Limits and refusals
Requests contain at most 256 KiB; individual reads and writes at most
64 KiB.
Write ranges and size updates are limited to 64 MiB.
Reads may seek beyond that limit and return EOF at the end of the file.
Paths are UTF-8, at most 4096 bytes, without NUL; components are limited
by the filesystem to 255 bytes.
A session holds at most 16 file or directory handles.
Directory replies carry at most 64 entries.
.Pp
APPEND, ownership or permission changes, timestamp preservation,
link operations, and vendor extensions are unsupported.
Operations that request unsupported attribute changes are refused before
applying any size change.
Preserving modes or times with client
.Fl p
options is not supported.
Recursive clients that request directory-mode restoration also receive
a refusal for that metadata update.
Legacy
.Nm scp
with
.Fl O
is not served.
Unsupported requests receive an SFTP status; malformed or oversized streams
may be closed after a refusal.
.Ss Interrupted uploads
An interrupted upload can leave a partial destination.
A successful writable close synchronizes the file before acknowledgment.
A disconnect ends the worker and releases its filesystem handles;
it does not make an upload transactional.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
An invalid protocol stream or filesystem stream error exits 1.
Unexpected command arguments exit 2.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr afterboot 8 ,
.Xr filesystem 7 ,
.Xr handles 7 ,
.Xr sha256sum 1
